  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Minimal Impacts Through Tuesday: Periodic light precipitation\n  and breezy westerly winds.\n\n- Mid-Week Valley Fog Potential: High pressure building on \n  Wednesday and Thursday increase the likelihood of valley \n  inversions.\n\n- Wetter and Mild Weekend: A Pacific system will tap into \n  atmospheric river moisture beginning late Friday.\n\nThrough Tuesday Night: A moist, zonal-to-southwesterly flow \nremains established across the Northern Rockies today. An initial,\nweak shortwave trough will cross northwest Montana this morning, \ngenerating scattered light showers. Snow levels remain around \n5,000 feet and above. While surface winds will remain relatively \nlight for most of the area, a tighter pressure gradient will \nsupport localized gusts of 15 to 20 mph from Lemhi County into \nsouthwest Montana.\n\nA more robust Pacific disturbance will traverse the region from \nthis evening through late Tuesday, expanding precipitation \ncoverage. Snow levels will be near 6,500 feet by Tuesday morning,\nensuring that valley precipitation will fall as rain. Westerly \nwinds will also increase on Tuesday, with valley locations seeing \nafternoon gusts of 15 to 25 mph. Given the high snow levels and \nlight QPF, overall sensible weather impacts through Tuesday night \nwill remain minimal.\n\nWednesday through Sunday: Transient upper-level ridging will \nbuild across the Intermountain West from Wednesday through \nThursday. This stabilizing air mass will foster the development of\nvalley inversions, leading to areas of low stratus and patchy \nfog. High temperatures during this timeframe will be heavily \ndependent on the persistence of this stratus deck; valley \nlocations that remain trapped under the inversion will struggle to\nwarm, while higher elevations enjoy clear skies and milder \nconditions.\n\nEnsemble guidance is in excellent agreement that the ridge axis \nwill migrate eastward by Friday. This shifts the Northern Rockies \ninto a southwesterly flow regime, which will effectively mix out \nany lingering valley inversions. Simultaneously, this southwest \nflow will tap into an Atmospheric River impacting the West Coast,\nadvecting a plume of anomalous moisture into the Inland Northwest.\nSnow levels will remain notably high through the weekend, generally\nholding between 6,000 and 7,000 feet. Overall precipitation amounts\nappear light to moderate in the mountains and very light in the \nvalleys as much of the precipitation is intercepted by the \nCascades.",
